, "watch": "A sustained, historic national shift toward Democrats by 2028, or a badly fractured Republican nomination, could narrow the margin.", "confidence": "high", "notes": ["No expert ratings published yet; forecast rests on fundamentals and polls.", "New or vacant seat with no expert ratings; fundamentals only.", "No usable general-election polls; forecast rests on fundamentals and expert ratings."]}, "overview": "Wyoming remains the most Republican state on the presidential map, with a partisan lean of roughly 23 points toward the GOP. The incumbent president is term-limited, so both parties will nominate new candidates, but no state polls or expert ratings are available for this race. The forecast rests on fundamentals, which point overwhelmingly to the Republican nominee.\n\nNational headlines describe Democratic gains in the 2026 midterms and voter discontent with the administration, but a shift of that size would need to be several times larger to put Wyoming in play. The state has voted Republican by wide margins in every recent presidential election, and its electorate is small, homogeneous, and resistant to national waves. The Democratic nominee would need a historic, uniform swing to compete here; the Republican side starts with a decisive structural advantage.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.01, "margin": -23.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.0232, "margin": -23.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.0282, "margin": -22.0}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.025, "margin": -23.0}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_dem": 0.0274, "margin": -22.0}, {"date": "2026-09-28", "p_dem": 0.0226, "margin": -23.0}, {"date": "2026-09-29", "p_dem": 0.0236, "margin": -23.0}, {"date": "2026-09-30", "p_dem": 0.0279, "margin": -23.0}]}